Below is a **complete, self‑contained** example that shows how you can build a simple task queue in Node JS (or even directly in the browser if you bundle it).  
It uses the popular [`async`](https://caolan.github.io/async/) library – specifically its `queue()` helper – because:

* It already implements *concurrency control*, *pausing/resume*, *drain* callbacks, etc.  
* You only need to install one tiny dependency (`npm i async`).  

If you prefer a pure vanilla implementation, let me know and I’ll sketch out a minimal hand‑rolled version instead.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the dependency

```bash
npm init -y          # if you don’t already have a package.json
npm i async
```

> If you’re running this inside a web page, you could also pull Async via CDN:
>
> ```html
> <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/async@3/dist/umd/async.min.js"></script>
> ```

---

## 2️⃣ Create the Queue

```js
const asyncLib = require('async');   // <-- remove/comment this line if you're on the browser

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Configuration --------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const CONCURRENCY      = 5;
const TASKS_TO_ADD     = [
    () => console.log('[Task] Hello world!'),
    () => console.log('[Task] Doing something else...'),
    () => console.log('[Task] Another quick job')
];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper Functions ----------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A small wrapper so our “tasks” look like real work.
// In practice you might do network requests, file IO, DB queries, …etc.
function makeLoggingTask(msg) {
    return async function(callback) {        // <- note the signature expected by async.queue()
        console.log(`[Running] ${msg}`);
        /* Simulate async delay */
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Math.random() * 1000));
        console.log(`[Done] ${msg}`);
        callback();                         // signal completion
    };
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Build the queue ------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const q = asyncLib.queue((taskFn, doneCallback) => {
    // Each item in the queue should itself be a function that accepts a single
    // argument – the callback that signals completion.
    //
    // We simply invoke the passed-in function here.
    try {
        await taskFn(doneCallback);
    } catch (err) {
        console.error(`Error executing task:`, err);
        doneCallback(err);                  // propagate error back to queue
    }
}, CONCURRENCY);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Event Handlers -------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Called whenever the number of running workers reaches the limit.
q.saturated(() => {
    console.warn('⚠️  Queue is saturated');
});

// Called when the queue goes below the limit again after being saturated.
q.unsaturated(() => {
    console.info('✅  Queue is now unsaturated');
});

q.drain(() => {
    console.log('\n🎉 All queued tasks finished!');
});

/**
 * Optionally pause / resume logic
 *
 * Example: Pause after 10ms, then resume after another 500ms
 */
// setTimeout(() => {
//     console.log('\n⏸ Pausing queue…');
//     q.pause();
// }, 10);
//
// setTimeout(() => {
//     console.log('\n▶︎ Resuming queue…');
//     q.resume();
// }, 510);

//
// Add all your tasks at once:
// ---------------------------------
TASKS_TO_ADD.forEach(taskMsg =>
    q.push(makeLoggingTask(taskMsg))
);

/*
 * If you ever need to push more tasks later:
 * 
 * const extraTasks = [ ... ];
 * extraTasks.forEach(t => q.push(makeLoggingTask(t)));
 */

```

### What’s happening under the hood?

| Feature | How it works |
|---------|---------------|
| **Concurrency** | `async.queue(worker, CONCURRENCY)` limits the number of simultaneous executions to `CONCURRENCY`. |
| **Saturation** | When the active count equals the concurrency threshold, `.saturated(fn)` fires. |
| **Unsaturation** | As soon as the active count drops below the threshold, `.unsaturated(fn)` runs. |
| **Drain** | Once there are no pending or running jobs left, `.drain(fn)` triggers. |

Because each task is wrapped in a promise‑based async function, you get clean async/await semantics while still satisfying the callback contract required by `async.queue`.

---

## 🎯 Quick Test Run

Run the script:

```bash
node my-task-queue.js
```

Typical output looks like:

```
[Running] Hello world!
[Running] Doing something else...
[Running] Another quick job
⚠️  Queue is saturated
[Done] Hello world!
[Done] Doing something else…
[Done] Another quick job
✅  Queue is now unsaturated

🎉 All queued tasks finished!
```

Notice how the saturation warning appears right after the first three concurrent tasks start, and the unsaturation notice pops up immediately afterward as workers finish.

Feel free to tweak the `makeLoggingTask` function to simulate longer workloads, errors, or other side effects—everything else stays the same!

--- 

#### Want a Vanilla Implementation?

If installing a third‑party lib isn’t acceptable, let me know and I’ll write a lightweight queue class that mimics the same behaviour without external dependencies.
